<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amami Ōshima</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Amami Oshima</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amami Ōshima is the largest of the Amami Islands, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a> and Okinawa, and is now part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagoshima-prefecture">Kagoshima Prefecture</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. It was brought under the Ryukyu Kingdom in the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma</a> seized it during its invasion of Ryukyu in 1609 and afterwards ruled it directly, exploiting it for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> production.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amami-oshima.jpg" alt="Amami Ōshima" width="320" height="277" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Image Science and Analysis Laboratory, NASA-Johnson Space Center. &quot;The Gateway to Astronaut Photography of Earth. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amami_Oshima_ISS019.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Amami Ōshima is the largest island of the Amami archipelago, about 380 kilometres south of the southern tip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyūshū</a> and 250 kilometres north of Okinawa. 35 square kilometres and is the seventh largest island of the Japanese archipelago after the four main islands, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okinawa-island">Okinawa Island</a> and Sado Island. It is of volcanic origin, with Mount Yuwanda, at 605 metres, its highest peak, and a coral reef surrounds its coast.</p><p>The East China Sea lies to the west and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a> to the east. The climate is humid subtropical, with frequent typhoons. It is home to rare endemic animals, among them the Amami rabbit, sometimes called a living fossil, and Lidth's jay.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>It is uncertain when the island was first settled. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">Stone tools</a> point to the Japanese Paleolithic, and pottery and other artifacts show constant contact with Jōmon, Yayoi and Kofun period <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nihon-shoki">Nihon Shoki</a> mentions it under the year 657. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara">Nara</a> and early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heian-period">Heian periods</a> it was a stopping place for envoys from Japan to the Tang court, and mother of pearl was an important export to Japan. It was briefly incorporated into the Ryukyu Kingdom as that kingdom expanded north along the archipelago.</p><p>Because Ryukyu was a tributary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of Ming China, this changed the island's status. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">Samurai</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimazu-clan">Shimazu clan</a> invaded in 1609 to counter Ming authority, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a> recognised the island as the domain's holding in 1624. Shimazu rule was harsh: the inhabitants were reduced to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> and forced to raise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> cane to meet high taxes, which often led to famine.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saigo-takamori">Saigō Takamori</a> was exiled to the island in 1859 and stayed for two years, and his house is preserved as a memorial museum. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a> it was incorporated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osumi-province">Ōsumi Province</a> and later became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagoshima-prefecture">Kagoshima Prefecture</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> the Amami Islands were occupied by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> until 1953. The island has about 73,000 inhabitants, living in the city of Amami, the towns of Tatsugō and Setouchi and the villages of Uken and Yamato.</p><p>Much of it lies within Amami Guntō National Park, established in 2017, and in 2021 it was listed as part of a serial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> with Tokunoshima, the northern part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okinawa-island">Okinawa Island</a> and Iriomote. Two dialects of the Amami language are spoken there, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> classifies the language as endangered.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Amami Ōshima” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They seized <b>Amami Oshima</b>, landed at Unten in northern Okinawa, took Nakijin and marched south while Naha's harbour defences held off an attack from the sea; King Sho Nei surrendered Shuri less than two weeks after the landing.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ryukyu-kingdom">Ryukyu Kingdom · Military</a></li></ul></section>
